Forms in HTML 
    Forms in HTML are used to collect user information
    Forms are block-level element ,while most of the form element inside them are in-line level.

Tags used on Forms:
    1. <form>
          It is a semantic element that defined the beginning of the form.
          It contains all the form-related element
            eg: <form action="/submit" method="post"></form>
    2. <label>
          Specifier the purpose of the input field
          Its linked to the <input> field using the for attributes ,which match the id of the input element
            eg: <label for="username">Name</label>
                <input type="text" id="username">
    3.<input>
          Used to collect user data.
          There are various type such as text ,email ,password ,number.
            eg: <input type="email" id="usedEmail">
    4.<select>
          Creates a dropdown  list from which users can select options.
            eg: <label for"skills">Skills:</label>
                <select id="skills:>
                    <option value="css">CSS</option>
                    <option value="html">HTML</option>
                </select>
    5.<option>
          Defines the option within a dropdown list.
    6.<fieldset>
          Groups form element together and provides a border around them.
            eg: <fieldset> 
                     <legend>Personal Information</legend> 
                     <label for="name">Name:</label> 
                     <input type="text" id="name"> 
                </fieldset>
    7.<legend>
          Provides a caption for the <fieldset> group.
    8.<textarea>
          User to collect multi-line text input.
          The rows and cols attributes specify the visible height and width.
            eg: <textarea rows="5" cols="30"></textarea>
    9.<button>
          Creates a click able button in the form. It can be used to submit or reset the form based on the type attributes.
            eg: <button type="submit">Submit</button>

Attributes of the <form> Tag:
    1.action : Specifies the URL Where form data will be submitted
        eg: <form action="/submit-data">
    2.method : Specifies how the data is sent to the server(GET or POST)
        GET- Sends data in the URL,not secure
        POST- Sends data Securely in the HTTP body.
          eg: <form method="post">
    3.autocomplete
        Defines whether form inputs should have auto-complete enabled(on or off)
          eg: <form autocomplete="on">

Attributes of the <input> Tag
    1.type : Specifies the type of input field (eg: text, email, password, etc..)
    2.id : Provides a unique identifier for the input element.
    3.name : Associates  a name with the input field ,used for form submission .
    4.value : Defines the initial value of the input field.
    5.required : Makes the field mandatory to fill before submission.
    6.readonly : Prevents user from modifying the input field value.
    7.disabled : Disables the input field.
    8.min ,max ,minlength ,maxlength : Used to define the minimum and maximum values or lengths for input.
    9.placeholder : Displays a hint text inside the input field.

Values of type Attribute in HTML <input> Tag
    The type attributes of the <input> tag in HTML determines the kind of input field displayed on the webpage. Here are the common value of the type attribute.
    1.text 
        Allows user to enter regular text.
        Default input type.
          eg: <input type="text">
    2.email
        Used for email input .It automatically validates the input for an email format.
          eg: <input type="email">
    3.password
        Display input as masked characters (dots or asterisks),hiding the user's input.
          eg: <input type="password">
    4.number
        Allows user to enter numerical values. It can have min, max, and step attributes for validation.
          eg: <input type="number" min="1" max="100" step="1">
    5.tel
        Allows users to input telephone numbers. it doesn't enforce specific validation but provides a numeric keyboard on mobile devices.
          eg: <input type="tel">
    6.radio
        Creates a radio button, allowing users to select one option from a group 
          eg: <input type="radio" name="gender" value="male">Male
              <input type="radio" name="gender" value="female">Female
    7.checkbox
        Creates a checkbox, allowing users to select multiple options independently
          eg: <input type="checkbox" value="subscribe">Subscribe
    8.button
        Create a click able button. Typically, you can use JavaScript to define what happens when it's clicked
          eg: <input type="button" value="Click Me">
    9.submit
        Creates a submit button that submits the form data to the server.
          eg: <input type="submit" value="Submit Form">
    10.reset
        Resets all the form fields to their default values.
          eg: <input type="reset" value="Reset Form">
    11.date
        Allows users to select a date using a date picker.
          eg: <input type="date">
    12.time
        Allows users to select a time.
          eg: <input type="time">
    13.week
        Allows users to select a specific week in a year
          eg: <input type="week">
    14.month
        Allows users to select a specific month and year
          eg: <input type="month">
    15.color
        Allows users to select a color from a color picker
          eg: <input type="color">
    16.image
        Creates a submit button in the form of an image
          eg: <input type="image" src="submit.png" alt="Submit">
    17.file
        Allows user to upload files from their device
         eg: <input type="file">
    18.range
       Creates a slider input that allows users to select a numeric value within a specific range.
         eg: <input type="range" min="1", max="100">

